 ## Overview
-Value-Noise FBM Texture Generation synthesizes procedural volumetric textures, primarily for cloud simulation, by summing multiple octaves of value noise with decreasing amplitude and increasing frequency [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It differs from gradient-based noise (e.g., Perlin) by using pre-computed random scalar values at grid vertices rather than gradient vectors, resulting in smoother, less detailed textures that are computationally cheaper to interpolate [S1, unofficial]. It is distinct from [Ridged Multifractal](4249-ridged-multifractal.md) which uses absolute values to create sharp peaks, and from [3D Perlin-Worley Noise Texture Generation](2355-3d-perlin-worley-noise-texture-generation.md) which combines gradient noise with distance-based cellular patterns.
