+- **Performance:** Value noise is generally faster than gradient noise because it avoids dot products and gradient vector storage, relying only on scalar interpolation [S1, unofficial]. However, it produces smoother, less detailed textures [S1, unofficial].
 - **Strengths:** Deterministic, easy to implement, smooth results suitable for clouds, low memory footprint for intermediate grids [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 - **Limitations:** Less directional detail than Perlin noise; 3D volume textures may suffer from lack of mipmap support in some formats/drivers (DDS) [S3, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Cloud rendering, terrain heightmaps, procedural textures where smoothness is preferred over sharp detail [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** When high-frequency directional detail is required (use Perlin/Simplex) or when mipmap support is critical for 3D volumes and the format does not support it [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Value-Noise FBM was popularized in procedural texture generation for computer graphics, particularly for cloud simulation. The specific implementation details cited here originate from voxel game development and early GPU-based cloud simulation research [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The use of value noise for clouds allows for efficient runtime generation on both CPU and GPU, with the FBM structure providing the necessary fractal detail to mimic natural cloud formations [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
